[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124445,
      "id": "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340",
      "title": "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7133d336-5c01-5c3b-85d2-224d74f079ed",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)= (y + 2)\\ln (x),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y + 2)\\ln (x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y + 2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y + 2)\\ln (x)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y + 2}{x} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y + 2}{x} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y + 2}{x}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y + 2=0,$ which means $y=-2.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x)=0,$ which means that $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(1,-2).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "3b3658a0-4d1a-5ecb-abc3-878ce59c4323",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
